# CONV tzif - the time zone information file format, RFC 8536
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Tier FORMAL: RFC 8536 is a published specification of the file format.
# The tests parse the files themselves (conv/lib/tzif.h); no tool or library
# reads them for the checks of 001 to 004. The zone database is found from the
# caller's TZDIR (conv/run.sh passes it as PC_CONV_TZDIR) or the common
# locations; NOTINUSE if there is none. Files whose version byte is beyond
# '3' are outside this RFC and are not judged. Every TZif file below the
# directory is checked (the whole database), a superset of the sample the
# task named (UTC, a fixed-offset zone, zones with transitions, version 2+).
#
# Deliberately not asserted: the reserved bytes of the header (the RFC says
# unused); the correctness of the zone data (that is the tz database's job,
# not the format's); leap-second files beyond the record rules; instants
# after the last transition other than the footer's agreement with it.

assertion  CONV.tzif.001
anchor     rfc8536.txt#3.1
anchor     rfc8536.txt#3
class      required
says       Every TZif file starts with the magic TZif and a version byte that is
           NUL, '2' or '3'; each header has a nonzero typecnt and charcnt and
           isutcnt and isstdcnt that are zero or equal to typecnt; a version 1
           file ends with its data block; a version 2 or 3 file continues with a
           second header of the same version, a data block that fits in the
           file, and a footer made of a newline, a TZ string without a newline
           or NUL, and a newline that ends the file.
test       t/001-layout.c
note       tier FORMAL (RFC 8536 sections 3 and 3.1, the MUSTs). class required:
           a failure is a conformance bug of the file or of its producer.
           Sources do not disagree.
nomutant   reads files; checked with a fixture database (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.tzif.002
anchor     rfc8536.txt#3.2
class      required
says       In both data blocks transition times are strictly ascending and
           every transition type index is below typecnt; no utoff is -2^31 and
           every isdst is 0 or 1; every designation index is below charcnt and
           a NUL follows it inside the designations; leap-second records start
           at a nonnegative time with a correction of 1 or -1, are at least
           2419199 seconds apart and their corrections differ by exactly one;
           indicators are 0 or 1 and a set UT indicator has its standard
           indicator set.
test       t/002-data.c
note       tier FORMAL (RFC 8536 section 3.2, the MUSTs). Sources do not
           disagree. Files that fail the layout rules are left to
           CONV.tzif.001.
nomutant   reads files; checked with a fixture database (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.tzif.003
anchor     rfc8536.txt#3.3
anchor     rfc8536.txt#3.3.1
class      required
says       The footer TZ string of a version 2 file follows the POSIX TZ syntax
           and a version 3 string follows it or the version 3 extensions; for a
           footer that is nonempty and a file with transitions, evaluating the
           string at the last transition gives the same offset, daylight-saving
           flag and abbreviation as that transition's type.
test       t/003-footer.c
note       tier FORMAL (RFC 8536 sections 3.3 and 3.3.1, the MUSTs). The
           consistency check evaluates the string with the system's localtime()
           under TZ set to it, so it is skipped for version 3 strings that use
           the extensions (a system need not implement them) and for a 32-bit
           time_t. Sources do not disagree.
nomutant   reads files; checked with a fixture database (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.tzif.004
anchor     rfc8536.txt#3.2
anchor     rfc8536.txt#4
class      disputed
says       Files follow the recommendations: they are not version 1; transition
           times are at least -2^59; utoff is within [-89999, 93599]; designations
           are 3 to 6 alphanumerics, plus or minus; the footer does not begin
           with a colon; the version 1 transitions are a contiguous run of the
           version 2+ ones.
test       t/004-recommendations.c
note       tier FORMAL but SHOULD only (RFC 8536 sections 3.2, 3.3, 4): a
           departure is not a conformance bug, so the class is disputed and the
           result is INSPECT. A first version 1 transition of exactly -2^31 is
           read as a range marker and left out of the subsequence rule (the
           text speaks of "time changes"); a person may read it the other
           way. Sources do not disagree on the rest.
nomutant   reads files; checked with a fixture database (conv/neg/run.sh)

assertion  CONV.tzif.005
anchor     rfc8536.txt#3.2
anchor     rfc8536.txt#3.3
class      required
says       With TZ set to a zone name, localtime() gives, at each of a spread of
           transitions of the zone's version 2+ data and one second before it,
           the offset from UT, the daylight-saving flag and the abbreviation
           that the file lists for that instant (the type of the transition, or
           of the previous one, type 0 before the first); for a zone without
           transitions and with a plain-offset footer, the footer's.
test       t/005-localtime-agrees.c
note       tier FORMAL (RFC 8536 section 3.2: which type applies to an instant;
           POSIX XBD 8.3 gives the meaning of TZ naming a file, which the RFC
           relies on). The test also sets TZDIR to the directory it found, which
           a system may ignore; then its own default database is compared with
           the found one and a mismatch is a deviation. The zones are: UTC and
           Etc/GMT+5 and Etc/GMT-14 (fixed offsets), America/New_York,
           Europe/London, Asia/Kolkata, Australia/Lord_Howe, America/Sao_Paulo,
           Europe/Paris; absent ones are skipped. Sources do not disagree.
